---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Download, ArrowUpRight, Clock, Flame, Snowflake } from '@lucide/astro';
import { env } from 'cloudflare:workers';
import { getData } from '../lib/data';
import { calendarWeek } from '../lib/historical';
import historicalData from '../data/historical-analogs.json';

const { snapshot } = await getData(env);
const weeks2026 = snapshot.weekly.data
  .filter(p => p.date.startsWith('2026'))
  .map(p => ({ ...p, week: calendarWeek(p.date) }));
const benchmarks = historicalData.benchmarks.map(b => ({
  ...b,
  extreme: b.weeks.reduce((a, p) => Math.abs(p.anomaly) > Math.abs(a.anomaly) ? p : a),
}));
const signed = (value: number) => (value > 0 ? '+' : '') + value.toFixed(1) + '°C';
const payload = JSON.stringify({ current: weeks2026, benchmarks }).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');
---

<Layout title="Historical ENSO Comparison: 1997, 2015, 2020 & 2023" description="Compare 2026 weekly Niño 3.4 anomalies with 1997, 2015, 2020 and 2023 using NOAA CPC data. See dated extremes and the limits of historical analogs." active="historical">
  <div class="page-shell subpage">
    <div class="page-intro"><div>
      <span class="eyebrow">HISTORICAL COMPARISON · WEEKLY NIÑO 3.4</span>
      <h1>Historical ENSO comparison.<br /><span>2026 against four benchmark years.</span></h1>
      <p>Compare weekly sea-surface temperature departures in the same NOAA CPC series and 1991–2020 baseline. Each line covers one calendar year, not a complete ENSO episode.</p>
    </div></div>

    <section class="trend-section analog-chart-section" aria-labelledby="analog-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">CALENDAR-YEAR TRAJECTORIES</span><h2 id="analog-heading">Niño 3.4 weekly anomaly progression</h2></div>
        <button class="icon-button download-analog-data" title="Download official historical analog data (CSV)" aria-label="Download official historical analog data as CSV"><Download size={17} /></button>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-toolbar analog-toolbar"><div class="analog-legend-pills">
        <span class="analog-current-pill"><i class="legend-dot current-glow"></i>2026 (Current)</span>
        {benchmarks.map(b => (
          <button class="analog-toggle-pill active" data-analog-toggle={b.id} aria-pressed="true" style={`--pill-color: ${b.color};`}>
            <i class="legend-dot" style={`background-color: ${b.color};`}></i>{b.shortName}
          </button>
        ))}
      </div></div>
      <div class="line-chart-wrap analog-canvas-wrap"><canvas id="analog-chart" role="img" aria-label="Interactive multi-line chart comparing 2026 weekly Niño 3.4 anomalies against benchmark years 1997, 2015, 2020, and 2023"></canvas></div>
      <script id="analog-data" type="application/json" set:html={payload} />
      <div class="chart-footer"><span>NOAA CPC WEEKLY DATA · 1991–2020 BASELINE</span><a href="/methodology/#indices">Niño 3.4 method <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a></div>
      <p class="figure-note">Weeks are numbered from January 1 in seven-day bins, including week 53 where present; month labels are approximate. Dates identify the center of each observation week. Similar curves do not predict the same regional weather.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="analog-profiles-section" aria-labelledby="profiles-heading">
      <div class="section-header-compact"><span class="eyebrow">DATED OBSERVATIONS</span><h2 id="profiles-heading">What each benchmark actually shows</h2><p>These are the largest warm departures or coldest departure within the calendar year shown. They are weekly observations, not seasonal ONI or RONI strength classifications.</p></div>
      <div class="analog-cards-grid">
        {benchmarks.map(b => (
          <article class={`analog-card ${b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'el-nino-' : 'la-nina-'}${b.id}`}>
            <div class="analog-card-header">
              <div class={`analog-card-icon ${b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'warm' : 'cyan'}`}>{b.category === 'el-nino' ? <Flame size={22} /> : <Snowflake size={22} />}</div>
              <div><span class="event-tag">{b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'Warm-year comparison' : 'Cold-year comparison'}</span><h3>{b.id} calendar year</h3></div>
            </div>
            <div class="analog-metric-row">
              <div><span class="analog-metric-label">{b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'WEEKLY MAXIMUM' : 'WEEKLY MINIMUM'}</span><span class="analog-metric-val">{signed(b.extreme.anomaly)}</span></div>
              <div><span class="analog-metric-label">WEEK CENTERED</span><span class="analog-metric-sub">{b.extreme.date}</span></div>
            </div>
            <p class="analog-card-text">{b.description}</p>
            <div class="analog-card-footer"><span class="analog-badge">{b.weeks.length} weekly observations</span></div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="analog-table-section" aria-labelledby="comparison-heading">
      <div class="section-header-compact"><span class="eyebrow">SAME DATASET · SAME BASELINE</span><h2 id="comparison-heading">Weekly benchmark summary</h2><p>All four stored series were checked against the CPC source on November 12, 2026. Values are rounded to one decimal place in the source.</p></div>
      <div class="table-container" role="region" aria-label="Weekly benchmark comparison" tabindex="0"><table class="analog-summary-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Calendar year</th><th scope="col">Weekly extreme</th><th scope="col">Week centered</th><th scope="col">Coverage</th><th scope="col">Observations</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{benchmarks.map(b => (
          <tr><td><strong>{b.id}</strong></td><td><strong>{signed(b.extreme.anomaly)}</strong> {b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'maximum' : 'minimum'}</td><td>{b.extreme.date}</td><td>{b.weeks[0].date} to {b.weeks.at(-1)?.date}</td><td>{b.weeks.length}</td></tr>
        ))}</tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="figure-note">Source: <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/data/indices/wksst9120.for" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NOAA CPC weekly SST and anomaly file</a>. A weekly extreme is not a three-month seasonal peak. The 2020 line excludes 2021–2023 and cannot describe the full multi-winter episode.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="method-section historical-limits" aria-labelledby="analog-limits-heading">
      <h2 id="analog-limits-heading">Does a stronger El Niño mean a wetter or snowier winter?</h2>
      <p>A larger Niño 3.4 anomaly describes a bigger ocean-temperature departure in that region. It does not specify rainfall, snowfall or storm counts at your location. Weekly conventional anomalies here also differ from the relative, three-month RONI used in CPC strength assessments.</p>
      <p>Historical analogs put the timing and size of an ocean signal in context. They are a small selected sample, not a probability forecast or a ranking of local damage. Other ocean patterns and atmospheric variability affect seasonal outcomes; individual storms and snow levels depend on shorter-range weather.</p>
      <p>For a North American winter question, start with the <a href="/winter-outlook/">dated U.S. winter outlook</a>, then the <a href="/impacts/california-snow-vs-rain/">California rain versus mountain snow</a> or <a href="/impacts/pacific-northwest-winter/">Pacific Northwest winter</a> guide. For other regions, see the <a href="/impacts/">regional impacts overview</a>. Use official local weather forecasts for travel and safety decisions.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">Interpretation reference: <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso_advisory/ensodisc.shtml" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CPC ENSO Diagnostics Discussion</a>, October 8, 2026 issue checked November 12. CPC notes that stronger events increase the likelihood of impacts without guaranteeing them. <a href="/forecast/#super-el-nino-heading">Compare weekly anomalies and seasonal strength</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="source-callout"><Clock size={20} /><div><strong>What does CPC expect next?</strong><p>The official phase probabilities are a monthly expert assessment informed by observations and models. They are separate from these historical curves.</p></div><a href="/forecast/">Official forecast <ArrowUpRight size={15} /></a></div>
  </div>
  <script>
    import { initCharts } from '../scripts/charts';
    initCharts();
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  .historical-limits p { margin-top: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--muted); }
  .figure-note { display: block; }
  .analog-summary-table { min-width: 780px; }
</style>
